      "title": "Review remaining XState opportunities in the web app",
      "description": "# Handoff: review remaining XState opportunities in the web app\n\n## Goal\n\nReview where XState should be adopted next in `apps/web`, without turning ordinary Svelte state or TanStack Query state into shallow machines.\n\n## Coordination note\n\nDo **not** include the Invitation Acceptance payment form in this review's implementation scope.\n\nOpenCode session `ses_f55506e01ffe4EFYsvbHBhWDHO` explicitly planned a component-scoped browser payment machine in `apps/web/src/lib/invitation-acceptance/payment-machine.ts` to replace the Stripe/form state juggling in `payment-form.svelte`. The exported session confirms that work was identified as its next browser-side machine. No `payment-machine.ts` exists in the current checkout yet, so treat it as owned by that session rather than completed.\n\nThe same session added or was adding the pure, actor-free server decision machine in `apps/web/src/lib/server/invitation-acceptance/decision.ts`. Do not use that request-local one-shot machine as the default shape for client workflows.\n\n## Recommended first candidate\n\n### Workshop express checkout\n\nFile: `apps/web/src/lib/components/workshops/workshop-express-checkout.svelte`\n\nThis is the strongest remaining temporal protocol:\n\n```text\nloading Stripe\n  -> creating payment intent\n  -> preparing payment element\n  -> ready\n  -> confirming payment\n  -> completing registration\n  -> succeeded\n                       \\-> failed -> retry/cancel\n```\n\nToday the protocol is spread across nullable Stripe resources, `isLoading`, `error`, `success`, two TanStack mutations, `onMount`, and an element-mounting effect (`:64-181`). More importantly, every rendered error retries by creating a new payment intent (`:192-206`), even when the failure may have happened after Stripe confirmation while completing registration. An explicit failed phase would make retry stage-aware.\n\nProposed direction:\n\n* Create a component-scoped actor with `useMachine`.\n* Model Stripe loading, payment-intent creation, confirmation, and registration completion as invoked actors.\n* Expose explicit `RETRY` and `CANCEL` events.\n* Keep Stripe objects ephemeral; do not persist the machine snapshot.\n* Make teardown stop/clean up Stripe element resources and ignore late async results.\n* Test the machine independently with provided fake actors, then add a small component wiring test. There is currently no direct test coverage for this component.\n\nThe component is conditionally mounted by `apps/web/src/lib/components/workshops/workshop-list.svelte:363-385`, so component lifetime is already a clean actor lifetime.\n\n## Conditional later candidate\n\n### Membership reactivation interaction\n\nFiles:\n\n* `apps/web/src/lib/components/ui/reactivate-member-dialog.svelte`\n* `apps/web/src/lib/components/ui/reactivate-membership-modal.svelte`\n\nThere is a genuine protocol across opening, saved-method preview, date/mode selection, amount recalculation, confirmation, missing-payment-method fallback, submission, and reconciliation. The container/modal interface is also wide.\n\nHowever, TanStack Query already owns preview, amounts, and mutation states. Do not add an XState layer that mirrors those statuses. Adopt XState only if it replaces the interaction orchestration wholesale with machine-invoked actors and materially narrows the modal interface. Existing browser tests and member-reactivation E2E tests provide a good safety net.\n\n## Explicit non-candidates for now\n\n* `workshop-event-modal.svelte`: keep lifecycle commands as TanStack mutations. Replace its bespoke copy flags with the existing `copy-button.svelte` module if appropriate; a copy-feedback machine is too small.\n* `notification-realtime.svelte.ts`: already presents the deep `connectNotificationRealtime(config) -> { destroy }` interface and has comprehensive tests. Reconsider only if visible connection state or manual retry is added.\n* Inventory item management, attendee management, and Discord Doctor: their many values are mostly independent controls. Prefer local discriminated state for coupled dialogs, such as Discord Doctor's `kickDialogOpen` plus `kickReview`.\n* Server-projected Invitation Acceptance rendering: Phoenix remains authoritative. Prefer typed presentation maps over a client actor for rendering statuses.\n* Coupon application, delayed navigation, copy feedback, and search debounce: use runes, existing query/remote-function primitives, or focused reusable modules.\n\n## Adoption rule\n\nUse XState when it removes a temporal protocol with mutually exclusive phases, state-dependent events, cancellation/retry, coordinated async work, stale-result protection, or meaningful lifecycle cleanup. Avoid machines that merely wrap independent values, one mutation, or presentation state.\n\nFor Svelte, keep machine definitions module-scoped and actor instances component-scoped. `@xstate/svelte` exposes `useMachine` (`snapshot`, `send`, `actorRef`) and `useSelector`; use `fromPromise` for state-owned async work and forward its `AbortSignal` where the underlying operation supports cancellation.\n\n## Review decisions requested\n\n1. Approve workshop express checkout as the first remaining client XState pilot.\n2. Confirm that its retry semantics should distinguish payment-intent creation, Stripe confirmation, and registration completion failures.\n3. Decide whether membership reactivation should remain unchanged or be considered later as a full orchestration replacement.\n\n## Workspace caution\n\nThe checkout already contains uncommitted Invitation Acceptance/XState work associated with GH-509. This audit made no repository edits. Preserve those changes and avoid overlapping the separate payment-form session.\n\n## Suggested skills\n\n* `codebase-design` — keep the machine interface deep and avoid layering a shallow state wrapper over existing query state.\n* `tdd` — specify checkout transitions and stage-aware retries before changing the Svelte component.\n* `implement` — carry out the approved pilot after review.\n* Official Svelte MCP — required for Svelte implementation and final component validation.",
